# SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Frederic Ruget <fred@atlant.is> (GitHub: @douzebis)
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

# One step per anomaly, numbered to match the `N.x.` headings the blob
# carries in its own string values, and always in the blob's own order:
# the unusual bytes first, the ordinary ones under them.
#
# Every heading is a top-level `name` line and every example is the
# submessage under it, so the blob's top level alternates the two and a
# wrapper's position is twice its anomaly's number.
#
# Where an anomaly has a canonical counterpart, how the two are shown
# depends on how far apart they are.  A `wire_lines` range is one
# contiguous run, so two adjacent lines take one step and one range; two
# lines with a `}` and a `field {` between them take a step each, since a
# range spanning them would drag those rows in as well.

title: Every anomaly in one blob

steps:
  - text: |
      anomalies.pb carries one example of every annotation prototext can
      emit. Read top to bottom it goes from bytes every parser accepts,
      through bytes that disagree with the schema, to bytes no parser can
      make sense of at all. This session was opened with

          protolens --type google.protobuf.FileDescriptorProto \
              tests/fixtures/anomalies.pb

      and picked up this commentary from anomalies.script beside the blob.

      Each example sits in a submessage of its own, under a heading line
      that stays readable however far the document is folded. Where an
      anomaly has an ordinary counterpart the two are written next to each
      other: the unusual line first, the same value spelled the ordinary
      way under it.

      Press space to hand navigation to this pane, then -> to step.
      Press w at any time to show the wire bytes under each line.
    fold: ["/ 1"]

  - text: |
      1. Legal, not canonical.

      1.a. A varint may carry trailing padding, and a field tag is a
      varint. This tag is written in three bytes where one would do. The
      line under it says the same thing with the tag every writer emits.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/2 Z"]
    node: /2/1
    wire_lines:
      from: /2/1
      to: /2/2

  - text: |
      1.b. A length prefix is a varint too, and can be padded the same
      way. The four bytes of length below describe a string that needs
      one — and again the next line is the same string, prefixed
      canonically.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/4 Z"]
    node: /4/1
    wire_lines:
      from: /4/1
      to: /4/2

  - text: |
      1.c. And so can a value. `number: 5` is one byte of information
      written in five: the 05 and four bytes of padding.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/6 1", "/6/1 Z", "/6/2 Z"]
    node: /6/1/2
    wire_line: /6/1/2

  - text: |
      1.c. The same 5, written the way every producer writes it. Same
      text, same value, one byte.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/6 1", "/6/1 Z", "/6/2 Z"]
    node: /6/2/2
    wire_line: /6/2/2

  - text: |
      2. Values that survive a round trip but not a re-encode.

      2.a. -1 as an int32 is specified to occupy ten bytes. This producer
      wrote five. It reads back as -1 everywhere.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/8 1", "/8/1 Z", "/8/2 Z"]
    node: /8/1/2
    wire_line: /8/1/2

  - text: |
      2.a. And this is what a re-encode would write: the same -1, sign
      extended to the specified ten bytes. Two spellings, one value —
      which is why the round trip is lossless only if the bytes are kept.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/8 1", "/8/1 Z", "/8/2 Z"]
    node: /8/2/2
    wire_line: /8/2/2

  - text: |
      2.b. There is one canonical NaN and 2^52 - 1 other ones. This is
      one of the others. Every arithmetic unit agrees it is NaN; no two
      of them agree on which.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/10 1", "/10/1 Z", "/10/2 Z"]
    node: /10/1/2
    wire_line: /10/1/2

  - text: |
      2.b. The NaN a re-encode would write. It reads identically, and it
      differs in exactly one byte — the one the row above marks.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/10 1", "/10/1 Z", "/10/2 Z"]
    node: /10/2/2
    wire_line: /10/2/2

  - text: |
      3. A newer producer against an older schema.

      3.a. The wire carries enum value 99. The schema this session was
      opened with has no name for it, so the number is all there is to
      show — 0x63, marked in the bytes below. The line under it holds a
      value the schema does have a name for.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/12 1", "/12/1 Z", "/12/2 Z"]
    node: /12/1/2
    wire_line: /12/1/2

  - text: |
      3.a. A value from the same enum that this schema does have a name
      for. Nothing about the bytes is different in kind: one varint, one
      number. All that changed is whether the schema could name it.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/12 1", "/12/1 Z", "/12/2 Z"]
    node: /12/2/2
    wire_line: /12/2/2

  - text: |
      3.b. The same disagreement, one step further: four fields the
      schema does not declare at all. Nothing is lost — the wire type is
      in the tag, so each one is still read, named by shape rather than
      by name. The tag's own bytes carry the field number, in the color
      the numeric key wears above them.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/14 Z"]
    node: /14/1
    wire_lines:
      from: /14/1
      to: /14/4

  - text: |
      4. A packed run.

      4.a. Three text lines. One wire record. The w row is what makes
      that visible: the bytes belong to the run, not to any one line of
      it. Two of the three are spelled the way sections 1 and 2 were: the
      middle element is a 0 padded to three bytes, and the last is a -1
      truncated to five. Both are marked where they sit in the run.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/16 1", "/16/1 Z"]
    node: /16/1/1
    wire_node: /16/1/1

  - text: |
      4.b. A second run holding the same three numbers, spelled the way
      the specification asks: one byte for the 0, and ten for the -1. One
      run against the other, same values — and the canonical one is the
      longer of the two, twelve bytes against nine.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/16 1", "/16/1 Z"]
    node: /16/1/2
    wire_node: /16/1/2

  - text: |
      5. The blob and the schema disagree.

      5.a. The schema says field 1 here is a string. The tag says varint.
      The tag wins — it is what the bytes actually are — and the field is
      shown by wire type instead. The three bits that disagree are the
      wire type digit at the front of the tag.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/18 Z"]
    node: /18/1
    wire_line: /18/1

  - text: |
      5.b. Declared a string, and it is a LEN record, so the shape is
      right. The payload is not valid UTF-8. The bytes are shown as they
      are, with the ill-formed sequence marked.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/20 Z"]
    node: /20/1
    wire_line: /20/1

  - text: |
      5.c. Declared a packed int32 field, and the payload does not decode
      as one. The last byte promises a byte that never comes, so the
      record is kept whole rather than guessed at.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/22 Z"]
    node: /22/1/1
    wire_line: /22/1/1

  - text: |
      6. Malformed wire bytes.

      From here on the bytes are not merely unexpected, they are
      unreadable — and each one takes the rest of its enclosing region
      with it. That is why every one of them sits alone in its own
      submessage: the length prefix is what bounds the damage.

      6.a. A length prefix claiming more bytes than the record has.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/24 Z"]
    node: /24/1
    wire_line: /24/1

  - text: |
      6.b. A varint with no terminating byte. The continuation bit of the
      last byte promises another one; there is none, so there is no way
      to know where the next tag would have started.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/26 Z"]
    node: /26/1
    wire_line: /26/1

  - text: |
      6.c. The same failure in a length prefix. Same reading in the
      bytes: the byte that promised, then the slot it promised.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/28 Z"]
    node: /28/1
    wire_line: /28/1

  - text: |
      6.d. A 64-bit field with only three bytes behind it.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/30 Z"]
    node: /30/1
    wire_line: /30/1

  - text: |
      6.e. A 32-bit field with only two. Different fields, one failure:
      the record runs off its own end, and the row counts what is
      missing.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/32 Z"]
    node: /32/1
    wire_line: /32/1

  - text: |
      6.f. Wire type 6 does not exist. Only the three type bits are
      wrong, so only they are marked — but a tag that cannot be
      interpreted means nothing after it can be found either.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/34 Z"]
    node: /34/1
    wire_line: /34/1

  - text: |
      6.g. Field number 0 is out of range: field numbers start at 1. The
      wire type is fine and is left alone; the field number beside it is
      not.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/36 Z"]
    node: /36/1
    wire_line: /36/1

  - text: |
      7. Groups.

      7.a. A group is opened by a tag and closed by a tag, with no length
      prefix anywhere. Nothing in this schema declares one, so all of
      these arrive as undeclared fields. This one's closing tag is padded
      — the tag_ohb of section 1, on the other end of the group.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/38 1", "/38/1 Z", "/38/2 Z"]
    node: /38/1
    wire_node: /38/1

  - text: |
      7.a. The same group, same field number, closed the ordinary way.
      The closing row is one byte where the one above it is two.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/38 1", "/38/1 Z", "/38/2 Z"]
    node: /38/2
    wire_node: /38/2

  - text: |
      7.b. Opened as field 100 and closed as field 101. A group's two
      ends have to agree, and nothing on the wire enforces it.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/40 1", "/40/1 Z"]
    node: /40/1
    wire_node: /40/1

  - text: |
      7.c. Closed with a field number no field may have — 2^29, one past
      the top of the range.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/42 1", "/42/1 Z"]
    node: /42/1
    wire_node: /42/1

  - text: |
      7.d. And a group that is never closed at all. The closing row has
      no bytes to show, so it shows the absence instead.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/44 1", "/44/1 Z"]
    node: /44/1
    wire_node: /44/1

  - text: |
      7.e. And last, an END_GROUP tag closing a group that was never
      opened.

      That is the whole vocabulary. Press space to take navigation back
      and explore on your own — the script does not constrain anything,
      so stepping back to any point picks up exactly where it left off.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/46 Z"]
    node: /46/1
    wire_line: /46/1
